#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Bootstrap an FDE ops repo for use with FDEstack skills

set -e

OPS_DIR="${1:-$HOME/fde-ops}"

if [ -d "$OPS_DIR/.git" ]; then
  echo "Ops repo already exists at $OPS_DIR"
  echo "Run: cd $OPS_DIR && claude"
  exit 0
fi

if [ -d "$OPS_DIR" ] && [ "$(ls -A "$OPS_DIR" 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
  echo "Error: $OPS_DIR exists and is not empty. Pass a different path:"
  echo "  bash init-ops-repo ~/my-fde-ops"
  exit 1
fi

mkdir -p "$OPS_DIR/customers"

cd "$OPS_DIR"
git init -q -b main
git commit -q --allow-empty -m "init: fde ops repo"

cat > .gitignore <<'EOF'
.DS_Store
*.swp
EOF

cat > CLAUDE.md <<'EOF'
# FDE ops repo

This repo holds customer engagement state for forward-deployed engineering work.
FDEstack skills read and write `customers/<name>/` and commit on every meaningful
event. The git history IS the engagement record.

## Skill routing

When the user's intent matches an FDEstack skill, invoke it via the Skill tool.
When in doubt, invoke the skill rather than answering directly.

Order of operations within a customer session:

1. **/customer-context <name>** — always first. Loads context, prints session
   brief, surfaces drift, asks what's new. Required before any other skill
   can run for that customer (they check the session marker).
2. **/discovery <name> <doc>** — process any inbound source (transcript,
   handoff notes, briefing, RFP, etc.). Extracts stack, stakeholders, real-
   vs-stated problem, open questions, conflicts.
3. **/scope <name> <problem>** — force binary success criteria. Vague items
   push to unknowns.md HIGH.
4. **/value-frame <name> <opportunity>** — dollar framing with lever taxonomy,
   sensitivity, defensibility check. Optional but recommended before /poc.
5. **/poc <name> [--purpose value|showcase|feasibility]** — throwaway proof.
   Mandatory learnings write-back at the end.
6. **/integrate <name>** — production rebuild. Cleanroom — does NOT read poc/.

Cross-cutting:
- **/triage [active|blocked|all]** — start-of-day portfolio view. No customer
   required. Surface highest-urgency customer.
- **/engagement-retro <name> [--since DATE]** — weekly per-customer retro.
   4 questions, audit trail for stale items, new unknowns get tagged.
- **/learnings [list|search|prune|edit|export]** — manage the cross-customer
   pattern library at ~/.fdestack/learnings.jsonl.

## Conventions

- Customer files live under `customers/<name>/` — 7 standard files plus dated
  artifacts (discovery-, scope-, value-frame-, retro-).
- Every skill commits to git. Commit messages follow `<skill>: <customer> <date>`.
- Vague items get pushed to `customers/<name>/unknowns.md` HIGH with audit-trail
  tags like `[scope-2026-05-19]` or `[value-frame-2026-05-19]`. They surface in
  every future /customer-context session brief.
- POC code at `customers/<name>/poc/` is THROWAWAY. /integrate refuses to read
  it. Learnings flow through stack.md / decisions.md / learnings.jsonl instead.

## What NOT to do here

- Don't manually edit context files mid-skill — let skills route. Edit between
  sessions is fine and surfaces as drift in the next /customer-context.
- Don't push this repo to a public remote. It contains customer info.
- Don't run gstack skills here — gstack is for code repos, FDEstack is for
  customer engagement repos. They peacefully coexist.
EOF

git add .gitignore CLAUDE.md
git commit -q -m "chore: add .gitignore + CLAUDE.md routing rules"

echo ""
echo "Ops repo ready at $OPS_DIR"
echo ""
echo "Next: start a customer session"
echo "  cd $OPS_DIR && claude"
echo "  /customer-context <customer-name>"
